<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">European Economic Area</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1994">1994</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as EEA</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The European Economic Area is an agreement that came into force in 1994, extending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>'s single market to member states of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-free-trade-association">European Free Trade Association</a>. Iceland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and, from 1995, Liechtenstein take part, adopting most EU single-market law without full membership. It allows small states such as Iceland and Liechtenstein to trade freely with the EU while staying outside it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/european-economic-area.jpg" alt="European Economic Area" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rob984 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:European_Economic_Area_member_states.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
